## Context
The order service needs to publish events that three other services read. We must pick a message system before the first release, and moving later means rewriting every producer and consumer.

## Options
- Kafka {chosen}
- Amazon SQS
- RabbitMQ {struck}

## Comparison
Table: Kafka, SQS and RabbitMQ
| Property | Kafka | SQS | RabbitMQ |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Ordering | Per partition | Only in FIFO queues | Per queue |
| Replay old events | Yes, by offset | No, deleted once read | No |
| Throughput | Very high | High, managed for you | Moderate |
| Running it | A cluster, or a managed service | Nothing to run | A broker to run |

## Pros and cons
Table: What each one gives and costs
| Option | Strength | Weakness |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Kafka | Replay and very high throughput | A cluster to operate and learn |
| SQS | No servers, simple to start | No replay, ordering costs throughput |
| RabbitMQ | Flexible routing | Lower throughput, one more broker to run |

## Decision
> [!decision] Kafka
> Three services need the same events, and a new one must be able to read the history. Only Kafka replays. We accept running a managed cluster.
